# Target C-II — outer-detour topology band

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Classification:** audited-working proof-only corollary; theorem-state authority
unchanged.  C-II remains open.

This note extracts the diameter-graph consequences of the audited outer-detour
floor.  It does not assume that the diameter graph is a forest; the forest
interpretation is stated separately through the canonical Woodall split.

## 1. Endpoint input

On the proof-only Section-118 common-cap endpoint, the outer-detour theorem gives

\[
 t:={q-k\over D}\ge S_*-o(1),
 \qquad S_*=2.798596312209462\ldots .
\tag{1}
\]

The loaded force-hull vertex count satisfies the elementary perimeter bound

\[
 {q\over D}\le\pi+o(1).
\tag{2}
\]

Here `k=d_H+s_Z`, `q=h+d_H`, and `s_Z,d_H\ge0` are the canonical diameter/rigidity
defects.

## 2. Defect and edge-density squeeze

Since `q/D=t+k/D`, (1)--(2) give

\[
 \boxed{\displaystyle
 {k\over D}\le \pi-S_*+o(1)<0.3444+o(1).}
\tag{3}
\]

Also `h=tD+s_Z`, so

\[
 \boxed{\displaystyle {h\over D}\ge S_*-o(1).}
\tag{4}
\]

For the canonical nonmatching excess `r_H=2h-q`,

\[
 {r_H\over D}
 =t+{s_Z\over D}-{d_H\over D}
 \ge t-{k\over D}
 =2t-{q\over D}.
\tag{5}
\]

Using (1)--(2),

\[
 \boxed{\displaystyle
 \liminf {r_H\over D}\ge2S_* -\pi
 =2.4543352775\ldots .}
\tag{6}
\]

For a conservative rational certificate, `S_0=2.7985` and `\pi<22/7` give

\[
 {k\over D}< {22\over7}-S_0={24105\over70000}<0.3444,
 \qquad
 {r_H\over D}>2S_0-{22\over7}>{2454\over1000}.
\tag{7}
\]

The stronger decimal in (6) is only a displayed evaluation of the exact symbolic
bound.

## 3. Forest interpretation

In the Woodall forest branch, `d_H` is exactly the number of caterpillar components
and the canonical identity is

\[
 r_H=\sum_{j=1}^{d_H}(q_j-2).
\tag{8}
\]

Thus any endpoint countersequence in the forest branch has at most
`(0.3444+o(1))D` diameter caterpillar components and at least
`(2.454+o(1))D` total excess beyond matching components.  This excludes a residual
forest made of only bounded-size caterpillars, but it does not force one component
to be macroscopic: the excess can be distributed over linearly many components.

The cyclic branch is not removed by this corollary.  There `d_H=0`, and the same
band says that the odd-unicyclic loaded diameter graph has `q=h` in the zero-
rigidity-defect subcase with `q/D\ge2.7985`; a further metric or source-minimality
argument is required.

## 4. Next exact target

The remaining forest obligation is now concrete:

\[
 d_H\le(\pi-S_*)D+o(D),
 \qquad
 \sum_j(q_j-2)\ge(2S_* -\pi)D-o(D).
\]

A closing theorem should charge this large caterpillar excess to the single common
positive-contact bridge block, or show that the resulting many-component interface
has a source-preserving strict diameter descent.  The scalar band itself remains
feasible and makes no C-II closure claim.
